{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of quiet that only exists at 5 AM, before the world remembers it is supposed to be busy. The air is cooler, thinner, as if night has washed it clean. I like to sit with a mug of coffee gone lukewarm, not because I forgot it, but because I was too absorbed in watching the light change.\n\nThe sun doesn't rise all at once. It leaks. First a pale thinning of navy into grey, then a blush of pink along the rooftops, then gold spilling over the edges of everything. Birds test their voices one by one, uncertain, then joining into a chorus like an orchestra tuning.\n\nIn those minutes, the day hasn't made any demands yet. No emails, no notifications, no deadlines tapping at the window. There is only possibility. You could be anyone in that light. You could start a novel, learn to bake sourdough, finally book that train ticket, or sit and do nothing at all, and all of those choices would be equally valid.\n\nWe spend so much of our lives rushing toward the next thing\u2014 the next hour, the next achievement, the next version of ourselves\u2014that we forget how to inhabit the present. But early morning reminds you that time isn't just something to spend or save; it's something to steep in, like tea leaves in hot water, slowly releasing flavor.\n\nMaybe happiness isn't a grand destination we arrive at, but these small, repeated pauses where we notice we are alive.",
